# Copy to .env.local for development.
# Only NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables reach the browser - never put a secret here.

# Leave the API URL unset for local development. The browser then talks to
# /api on its own origin, which next.config.ts proxies to the API (see
# API_ORIGIN there). Setting NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL makes every request
# cross-origin: the SameSite session cookie is dropped and CORS blocks the
# call, which looks exactly like a broken login.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.example.org

NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME=Consent Portal

# Must match the backend's csrf_header_name / csrf_cookie_name.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CSRF_HEADER=X-CSRF-Token
NEXT_PUBLIC_CSRF_COOKIE=cmp_csrf

# Where staff sign in. A staff account that lands here is pointed there.
NEXT_PUBLIC_STAFF_PORTAL_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Origins the dev server may serve its own assets and HMR socket to, on top of
# localhost and 127.0.0.1. Needed only when reaching this machine by IP or
# hostname from another device; Next blocks its dev resources otherwise and the
# app then misbehaves without saying why. Comma-separated. Development only.
# DEV_ORIGINS=192.168.1.42,my-laptop.local

# The key service, for decrypting personal data this portal is served as
# ciphertext. Server-side only, and NOT NEXT_PUBLIC_: the browser must not know
# where it is, let alone reach it. The page asks /dkms/decrypt on this origin,
# and that handler - which runs on the server and can read the HttpOnly session
# cookie - asks the service.
#
# Three things have to hold for a value to open, and each failure looks the
# same on the page - `SE::...` where a name should be - so check them in order:
# 1. The same service, or one holding the same master key, that the platform
#    API encrypts with (its DKMS_URL). A different key opens nothing.
# 2. Reachable from *this server*: `curl <DKMS_URL>/health` on the machine
#    running the portal. A service bound to 127.0.0.1 is not reachable by IP
#    from anywhere else - set its HOST to the interface callers use.
# 3. Restart the portal after changing this: it is read when the server
#    starts. The portal's own log then says `[dkms] ... unreachable` or
#    `answered <status>` if either check fails.
# The key service this portal decrypts with - the one the platform API sealed
# the data with. Typically on another host on the LAN; the route posts to
# ${DKMS_URL}/bulk_decrypt. Required: there is no fallback.
DKMS_URL=http://<ip>:32688
